![плавание кролем](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/f65c7a07-e3bc-407d-8f73-b5bed5b11879)

{{% steps %}}

### Step 1
Arm movement – Catch:
Bend your wrist and elbow slightly forward to "catch" and grip the water in front of you.

### Step 2
Pull and Push:
Pull your arm back under your body toward your hip. Your elbow stays high as your hand pushes water back past your thigh.

### Step 3
Arm Exit:
Lift your elbow out of the water first, followed by your hand. Keep your arm loose and relaxed during this phase.

### Step 4
Recovery:
Carry your relaxed arm forward over the water with a high elbow, preparing to place it back in front of you.

### Step 5
Entry and Reach:
Slide your hand smoothly into the water fingertips first, followed by your wrist and elbow, then reach forward to glide.

### Step 6
Most of the pulling power comes from your back and shoulders, supported by your forearms and hands.

### Step 7
Leg movement (Flutter kick):
The legs do not generate as much forward speed as the arms; their main role is to keep your body balanced and horizontal in the water.

### Step 8
Kick continuously in an alternating up-and-down motion: as one leg kicks down, the other rises.

### Step 9
Kick from your hips with a whip-like motion, rather than just bending your knees.

### Step 10
Keep your knees mostly straight (with only a slight bend). Point your toes back and keep your feet close together so your big toes almost brush past each other.

### Step 11
Breathing:
Take a quick breath in through your mouth by turning your head to the side as that arm recovers over the water. Turn your face back into the water as the arm enters.

### Step 12
Exhale smoothly underwater through your nose (or nose and mouth when swimming faster) so your lungs are ready for the next breath.

### Step 13
You can take a breath every two strokes (to one side) or every three strokes (alternating sides).

{{% /steps %}}

{{< callout type="info" >}}
⠀﻿﻿Avoid these common mistakes:
⠀﻿﻿• Tensing your arm in the air: keep your recovering arm completely relaxed to save energy and protect your shoulders.
⠀﻿﻿• Breathing too late: turn your head smoothly with your body rotation before the arm enters the water so you have enough time to breathe.
⠀﻿﻿• Incorrect kick depth: kicking too deep creates drag, while kicking too high above the surface wastes energy.
⠀﻿﻿• Lifting your head up instead of rotating to the side: lifting your head drops your hips and strains your neck. Look down at the bottom of the pool.
⠀﻿﻿• Swimming completely flat without body roll: rolling your shoulders and hips along with each stroke reduces water drag and makes breathing much easier.
⠀﻿﻿• Flexing your feet: keep your toes pointed backward instead of flexing your ankles up.
{{< /callout >}}
